      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a deck build in vancouver. using western red cedar. can u explain the main difference between live load and dead load for the sizing calcs? need to make sure my post spacing is right.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dead load and live load in your deck build are the two main forces you need to account for when sizing your posts and beams. The **dead load** is the weight of the structure itself—think the lumber, the decking boards, and any fixed components. In contrast, the **live load** is the temporary, variable weight, like people walking on the deck, furniture, or snow accumulation. You need to calculate both to ensure your posts are strong enough to handle the total expected weight, which is crucial for proper post spacing.",
