    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "makes sense. what kind of numbers am i looking at for the loads though? like do i just guess the weight of the people or is there a standard psf (pounds per square foot) i should use for residential decks in bc? also how do i account for the weight of the cedar itself in the dead load calc?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For residential decks in British Columbia, you should definitely not guess the weight of people; the local building code dictates standard live loads, which are typically around 40 psf for decks. You'll need to consult the specific BC building code for the precise required live load, but 40 psf is a common benchmark. To calculate the dead load from your cedar, you'll need to look up the specific weight per board foot for Western Red Cedar, which varies slightly based on species and moisture content, and then calculate the total weight of all your materials per square foot. This calculation is a bit more involved, but it's the only way to get an accurate picture of the structure's own weight.",
